    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Ford Rolls Out the Ranger MS-RT Champion Edition: You Can Choose Any Color As Long as It Is Black\n1970 Chevrolet Nova Yenko Deuce Flaunts LT1 Power, One of 122 Made\n17 photos\nPhoto: Mecum Auctions\nDon Yenko is primarily known for dropping 427-cubic-inch (7.0-liter) L72 engines in Chevrolet Camaros , but his shop modified other vehicles as well. Yenko actually started out with the Corvair in 1966 and added the Chevelle and Nova to its portfolio in 1969. In 1971-1972, Yenko also built a beefed-up version of the Vega.\nYenko put together two different iterations of the Nova. In 1969, the dealership used the same L72 engine it fitted in the Camaro. Rated at a whopping 425 horsepower, the big-block V8 turned the tiny Nova into a beast. Only 38 of these \"Supernovas\" were built, and fewer than 10 are known to exist today.\nWith big-block V8 engines already expensive to insure, Yenko switched to a small-block powerplant in 1970. The dealership's unit of choice was the LT1, a 350-cubic-inch (5.7-liter) V8 usually found in the Camaro Z28 and Chevrolet Corvette. The mill was rated at 360 horsepower; it was more potent than many big-block V8s available at the time.\nNicknamed the Deuce, the 1970 Yenko Nova was produced in notably more units than its 1969 L72 predecessor, but \"common\" is not the word we're looking for here. That's because only 175 examples rolled out of Don Yenko's shop. They're obviously desirable today and a rare sight out in the open.\nIf you haven't seen one recently, a Cortez Silver example was recently listed among the lots of Mecum's upcoming Las Vegas 2026 auction. It's in pristine condition, well documented, and equipped with a four-speed manual gearbox. The latter makes this Deuce one of 122 units built with this setup; the other 53 examples have the three-speed automatic gearbox.\nThis Nova is a true COPO car. Don Yenko utilized COPO sequence 9010 to order the factory-installed LT1 V8 engine. The COPO 9737 process added other features required to turn the Deuce into a proper sports car. The mill mates to a Positraction rear end with a 4.10 ratio, while the car rides on a heavy-duty suspension.\nThe Nova is obviously loaded with goodies that set it apart from a regular factory model. The list includes an exclusive Hurst shifter, a hood-mounted tach, Yenko-specific alloy wheels, and \"sYc\" markings ( Yenko Super Car ) on he front seats.\nRotisserie-restored to factory specifications, the Deuce wears the factory-correct Cortez Silver paint, along with black stripes running across the hood, the sides, and the trunk.\nThe car is absolutely flawless. The paint shines like it was just applied, and the interior doesn't show an inch of wear and tear. The engine? Well, you could basically eat off the air cleaner.\nIt also comes with a huge folder of valuable paperwork . The car is well documented by the COPO Connection and has a letter of authenticity plus a Yenko Vehicle Verification Certificate. It doesn't get any better than this.\nHowever, there's a catch. The car was actually re-bodied, meaning that it was likely rescued after many years of neglect, and its original sheet metal was consumed by rust. This probably explains why it sold for only $82,500 back in 2009.\nBut that was a very long time ago, so this Deuce will probably fetch a six-figure sum in 2026. As of this writing, the world's most expensive Deuce Nova is a Fathom Blue example that sold for $230,000. Two more cars fetched a little over $150,000 in recent years. It will go under the hammer on November 14.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nChevrolet Nova 1970 Chevrolet Nova chevrolet yenko nova Yenko Deuce Chevrolet classic car muscle car rare\nAbout the author: Ciprian Florea\nAsk Ciprian about cars and he'll reveal an obsession with classics and an annoyance with modern design cues. Read his articles and you'll understand why his ideal SUV is the 1969 Chevrolet K5 Blazer. Full profile\nUP NEXT Someone Bought a BMW X5 for Less Than $4K in the States",
